<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Civil disobedience</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Civil disobedience is the deliberate, non-violent refusal to obey certain laws or government demands as a form of political protest. It was used on a mass scale by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> movement against British rule. These campaigns contributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-independence">Indian independence</a> in 1947, a decisive step in the dissolution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/civil-disobedience.jpg" alt="Civil disobedience" width="320" height="456" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gandhi_at_Dandi,_5_April_1930.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The practice is older than the term. An early depiction is in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sophocles">Sophocles</a>' play Antigone, in which Antigone defies King Creon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>, who forbids her to give her brother Polynices a proper burial, and says she must obey her conscience rather than human law. The earliest recorded collective incident took place under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, when unarmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> gathered in the streets to prevent the installation of pagan images in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Temple in Jerusalem</a>. Conrad Grebel and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anabaptism">Anabaptists</a> advocated civil disobedience to oppression, and Étienne de La Boétie, in his Discours de la servitude volontaire of 1552, was one of the first to propose non-cooperation as an effective weapon.</p><p>Henry David Thoreau's essay Resistance to Civil Government, first published in 1849 and published after his death in 1866 as Civil Disobedience, popularized the term in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Mahatma Gandhi wrote in 1935 that he had not drawn the idea from Thoreau, since resistance to authority in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> was well advanced before he read the essay, and that he came to prefer the phrase civil resistance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Civil disobedience is the active and professed refusal of a citizen to obey certain laws, demands or orders of a government or other authority. Some definitions require it to be nonviolent, as Black's Law Dictionary does, while others, such as that of Christian Bay, require only carefully chosen and legitimate means. Howard Zinn noted that some campaigns classed as nonviolent, such as the Birmingham campaign, included elements of violence. Writers also distinguish revolutionary from non-revolutionary disobedience, and collective from solitary acts such as Thoreau's.</p><p>It concerns a citizen's relation to the state, not a conflict between two public bodies. Those who practise it can expect arrest, charge and punishment. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr">Martin Luther King Jr.</a> held that a person who breaks a law that conscience tells him is unjust, and accepts the penalty, expresses the highest respect for law. Acts that avoid punishment by concealment or flight, or a test case brought only to challenge a law's constitutionality, are generally not counted.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Governments have generally not recognized civil disobedience as legitimate. The law usually distinguishes criminal motive from criminal intent, so a protester with admirable motives may still have a criminal intent, and the saying goes that any justification must come from outside the legal system. It has been used by many movements: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susan-b-anthony">Susan B. Anthony</a> in the late 19th century, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saad-zaghloul">Saad Zaghloul</a> in the 1910s, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> in 1920s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, and the civil rights movement in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in the 1960s, in which King and James Bevel led protests. The term has also been stretched; in Marshall Cohen's words it has been used to describe everything from bringing a test case in court to taking aim at a federal official.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Civil disobedience” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It was achieved through the mass <b>civil disobedience</b> campaigns led by Gandhi and the Indian National Congress and the growing impossibility of governing some 400 million people who no longer consented to be governed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Republic of India was the outcome of a national movement that had challenged British rule since the founding of the Indian National Congress in 1885 and, under Mahatma Gandhi from 1920, mobilised millions through campaigns of non-cooperation and <b>civil disobedience</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-india">Republic of India · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The ANC’s Programme of Action of 1949 called for boycotts, strikes and <b>civil disobedience</b>, and in 1955 the Congress Alliance, which joined the ANC with Indian, coloured and white congresses and a trade union federation, adopted the Freedom Charter.</blockquote><a href="/movement/anti-apartheid-movement">Anti-apartheid movement · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Civil disobedience</b> spread across India, and over 60,000 people were jailed; at Peshawar troops fired on unarmed followers of Abdul Ghaffar Khan.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>After 1918 Germany’s colonies and the Ottoman Arab provinces became League of Nations mandates, and resistance grew, from Mahatma Gandhi’s campaigns of <b>civil disobedience</b> in India to risings against conscription and taxes in Africa.</blockquote><a href="/government/colonial-rule">Colonial rule · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>The gesture launched a mass campaign of <b>civil disobedience</b> across India.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-6">6 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
